# Mule Flow & Logic Generation Intent

When the user's request is about **generating**, **creating**, **scaffolding**, **building**, or **implementing** Mule flows, sub-flows, DataWeave transformations, or integration logic, use this intent to guide the process.

---

## STEP 0 — Identify Integration Pattern (do this FIRST, before reading any files)

Read the requirements, RAML spec, or Confluence design from `project/input_develop/` and determine which integration pattern applies. State the identified pattern explicitly before proceeding.

| Pattern | Use when | Template directory |
|---------|----------|--------------------|
| Pattern 3.2 — Anypoint MQ consumer | API subscribes to a queue (MANUAL ack, circuit breaker, DLQ routing) | `agents/develop/templates/mq-proc-api-template/` |
| Pattern 3.1 — HTTP proc API | Synchronous HTTP listener, APIKit router, downstream sys API calls | `agents/develop/templates/http-proc-api-template/` |
| Pattern 3.3 — Salesforce PubSub | Subscribes to a Salesforce platform event channel | `agents/develop/templates/sf-pubsub-exp-api-template/` |
| SF HTTP sys API | HTTP listener calling Salesforce composite/REST API | `agents/develop/templates/sf-http-sys-api-template/` |

---

## MANDATORY PREFLIGHT — Read ALL files below before generating a single line

**STOP. Do not generate any XML, DWL, POM, or properties until every file in this list has been read.**

### From the matched template directory — read ALL of these:

- `pom.xml` — **canonical dependency list**; it only declares what the parent POM does NOT already provide; copy its structure exactly, do not add or remove dependencies without explicit justification
- `src/main/mule/{template-name}.xml` — canonical flow/consumer structure, namespace declarations, `xmlns:doc`, error-handler wiring
- `src/main/mule/common/global-config.xml` — canonical global config: property loading, `<configuration defaultErrorHandler-ref>`, HTTP listener, APIKit, AKV, logger, notification, MQ, circuit breaker, Object Store configs
- `src/main/mule/common/global-error-handler.xml` — canonical error handler branches, `module-error-handler-plugin:process-error`, DLQ, notification, `check-for-raise-error-sub-flow`
- `src/main/mule/common/common-connectivity.xml` — canonical HTTP request flow: `until-successful`, `success`/`errorDetails` pattern, `vars.targetApiParams.requestMethod / requestPath / requestPayload / headers / queryParams / uriParams`
- `src/main/mule/common/common-utility.xml` — canonical utility sub-flows: `responding-ack-sub-flow`, `common-log-integration-error-details-sub-flow`, `publish-to-dlq-sub-flow`, `send-notification-sub-flow` (`module-notification:call-notification-api`), `success-response-sub-flow`
- `src/main/mule/implementation/*.xml` — all sub-flow files (canonical patterns for schema validation, identity resolution, scatter-gather, result handling)

### From rules — read ALL of these:

- `agents/develop/rules/implementation-ruleset_v4.mdc` — **read in full, all sections**
- `agents/review/rules/review-pom-maven-standards.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-mule-global-config.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-error-handling.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-logging-standards.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-dataweave-standards.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-properties-config.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-security-standards.mdc`
- `agents/review/rules/review-mule-flow-naming.mdc`

**Do not start generating until every file above has been read.**

---

## Procedure

1. Complete Step 0 (pattern identification) and the Mandatory Preflight above
2. **Check `project/input_develop/` for pre-fetched JIRA and Confluence context files:**
   - **If `jira-*.md` and `confluence-design-*.md` are both present:** Read them immediately to understand the full business requirements, acceptance criteria, downstream systems, and Confluence design decisions. Do NOT re-fetch from JIRA or Confluence.
   - **If either or both files are missing:** Inform the user which file(s) are absent and ask: *"No pre-fetched JIRA/Confluence context was found in `project/input_develop/`. Would you like me to fetch it now? If yes, please provide the JIRA story ID."*
   - **If the user provides a JIRA story ID:** Fetch the JIRA story and its linked Confluence page, then save `jira-{STORY-ID}.md` and `confluence-design-{STORY-ID}.md` to `project/input_develop/` before proceeding.
   - **If the user says to proceed without:** Continue using only the RAML spec and any other files present in `project/input_develop/`.
3. **Understand the integration requirements** from the pre-fetched context files, RAML spec, or user's description
4. **Generate Mule artifacts** following the standards — every generated file must match the template structure read in the Mandatory Preflight

## Generation Scope

- Mule XML flow files under `src/main/mule/`
- Global configuration in `src/main/mule/common/global-config.xml`
- Error handler in `src/main/mule/common/global-error-handler.xml`
- DataWeave scripts under `src/main/resources/dwls/`
- Error mappings under `src/main/resources/errors/`
- Property files under `src/main/resources/properties/`
- `log4j2.xml` configuration

## Handoff — After Generation

After all files have been written, suggest the next step to the user. Do **not** invoke it automatically:

```
Generation complete. To validate the build, run /use-build in a new chat.
It will run mvn clean package with the Insulet canonical VM args and auto-fix any
schema, POM, property, or DataWeave compile errors it finds.
```
